strings.NTG_presmuta.14.d:
Unlike most other days, Sedov was relatively enthusiastic for the meeting that was scheduled to begin in a few moments, and who wouldn't be? The Economic plans, or as the man himself nicknamed it, “Preobrazhensky’s Plan”, had been a sweeping success in almost every aspect. Everywhere Sedov looked the economy was not only strengthening, but also surpassing the neighboring warlord economies, truly a sign of the bountiful Soviet future to come.

Sedov was busy in these idealistic thoughts when Preobrazhensky finally entered the room along with one of his aides, holding various documents and charts detailing this past year's growth. Preobrazhensky began the meeting with a speech;

“Gentlemen, over the past few months the entire state of Nizhny Tagil has been through a positive overhaul of various institutions. We have seen our industrial and material yields increase dramatically, with some economic centers reporting nearly triple the amount of productivity in their factories. Our economic reforms have drastically increased employment and wages for the average soviet man and woman, with poverty rates decreasing across the board.”

The room roared with applause from all the members of the soviet inner circle, with Sedov himself nearly bursting from the emboldenment he felt in this success, this tide of pride kept on going as Preobrazhensky continued;

“While there is still a long way to go in reclaiming the motherland, this demonstrates that our combined policies and plans will surely deliver us a near Utopian state, that is all, and Long Live the Union!”, Preobrazhensky said, with the last noble words resounding off of the walls with the rest of the state members repeating them.
